A||||9||Shipbuilder||Hurricane Floyd||||9
Z||000000||Shipbuilder||09-24-1999||08:35 PM||||Okay guys, I need help.<BR>My buddy's 86 GT became a victim of hurricane Floyd. 30" of water in the garage.(fresh water) I have told him to dry the car out completely. Change the oil and filter. Remove the air intake tubing and make sure it is clear. Pull all the plugs and crank it over to blow out any water that may have gotten in through the exhaust.<P>What should be done with the automatic transmission? <BR>Now come the electronics. Do we dry out everything and try it?<BR>What am I missing?<BR>Any ideas are greatly appreciated.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Cooter||09-24-1999||09:36 PM||||I have built a few flood cars before and they are usually Ok if it was fresh water. YOu are right on pulling plugs and changing the oil, auto trans is a different story. If the engine has not been turned over, the water should not have gotten into the converter, so a couple of fluid and filter changes should take care of it. Some other considerations: remove and drail fuel tank, pull seats and all carpet, remove radio and heater controls and other electronics including the ECM and use a good grade contact cleaner on all connections. Follow this with a coating of the anti corrosion contact grease. THe car will probably have "gremlins" due to the fact that water has gotten in places where it should never go. Some damage may not show up for a while- like alternator, starter, headlight motors, rusted e-brake cables and sticking calipers. Good Luck.||||reg||9
Z||000002||Eddie||09-24-1999||09:50 PM||||YOU MAY WANT TO SPRAY SOME WD40 DOWN THE PLUG <BR>HOLES  PULL THE VALVE COVERS SPRAY THE VALAES REAL GOOD <BR>	GOOD LUCK EDDIE||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-24-1999||09:51 PM||||EXACLY where on the car did the water come up to? Exactly as posible. It makes a diferance in how bad this is. <P>If the water was up to the top of the console you need to dry out the ECM before you start the car. If it soaked the dash you need to dry that out too. Flush everything with clear water then dry completely. let things sit in a 90-110 degree oven for at least an hour if posible. or use a hair dryer. be carefull with the hair dryer.<P>DO NOT start the car untill the tranny is drained. Otherwise it will distribute the water thru the whole tranny. Right now the water is mostly sitting there doing nothing.<P>To drain the tranny just buy a filter kit, pull the pan, and install the kit. I'm not sure if or where the TH125 has a vent. How much would have gotten in depends on how long it was under and the size/design of the vent opening.<P>If you started the car, water is now all thru the tranny. how much depends on just how much soaked into it. could be allot or very little.<P>In this case, you need to flush the tranny untill you are sure all the water is out. Disconect the BOTTOM cooler line AFTER changing the pan filter to remove standing water. Connect a scrap plastic line to the fitting. then pour in fresh fluid, about 2-3 quarts and start the car. let the line drain into a CLEAN pan that you can watch. start the car and let it flow some oil out. check the pan and see if there is any water. If there is run the engine and keep pouring in more fluid untill it's clean. run the engine in short cycles, and empty the pan in between.<P>If the water got up to the fuel tank filler then you need to check the tank. If the gas cap was tight very little should have gotten in. In that case a few full tanks with "dry gas" in each will probably get rid of it.<P>If allot got in you need to syphon out the bulk of it. then the dry gas can get the last bits.<P>If you tried to start the car with to much water in, then there is now water in the fuel lines. You need to pull the lines apart and blow them out. replace the fuel filter when you do this.<P>pull the interior appart. seats, carpet, door pannels. hang them where they will get lots of air and sun. If you don't you'll get mildew and rust like you can't believe. let the sound dedener under the back half the console dry out good to.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-24-1999||09:54 PM||||unless the water got as high as the dash middle, which is about the same hight as the cyl head. little if any water got into the motor.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shipbuilder||09-25-1999||09:54 AM||||Thanks to all replies;<BR>The water came to just below the speedometer.<BR>Haven't cranked the engine at all. Will follow your suggestions and let you know how it comes out.<BR>Thanks again.<BR>Pete||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dan||09-25-1999||06:39 PM|||| At all electrical connections use contact cleaner first the a di-electric grease like silicon grease to help displace moisture and <BR>and prevent corrosion. It's doable.||||reg||3
Z||000007||Cooter||09-25-1999||07:39 PM||||One last thought- wheel bearings and CV joints may need to be disassembled and cleaned.||||reg||11
Z||000008||theogre||09-26-1999||06:37 PM||||good point.... if there are any holes in the CV boots they would also need work. Wheel bearings would get at least a little... the caps almost always leak at least a little.<P>pull the console and check the ECM. take off the ECM covers and check it's guts.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Harry||09-27-1999||02:04 PM||||You can believe me when i say,<BR>If it has a seal-theres water in it!!!!!<BR>||||reg||8
